{"id":"workloads-pods-init-containers","corpus":"k8s","title":"Init Containers","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Init Containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"This page provides an overview of init containers: specialized containers that run before app containers in a Pod. Init containers can contain utilities or setup scripts not present in an app image."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can specify init containers in the Pod specification alongside the containers array (which describes app containers)."},{"kind":"p","text":"In Kubernetes, a sidecar container is a container that starts before the main application container and continues to run. This document is about init containers: containers that run to completion during Pod initialization."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Understanding init containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"A Pod can have multiple containers running apps within it, but it can also have one or more init containers, which are run before the app containers are started."},{"kind":"p","text":"Init containers are exactly like regular containers, except:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Init containers always run to completion."},{"kind":"li","text":"Each init container must complete successfully before the next one starts."},{"kind":"p","text":"If a Pod's init container fails, the kubelet repeatedly restarts that init container until it succeeds. However, if the Pod has a restartPolicy of Never, and an init container fails during startup of that Pod, Kubernetes treats the overall Pod as failed."},{"kind":"p","text":"To specify an init container for a Pod, add the initContainers field into the Pod specification, as an array of container items (similar to the app containers field and its contents). See Container in the API reference for more details."},{"kind":"p","text":"The status of the init containers is returned in .status.initContainerStatuses field as an array of the container statuses (similar to the .status.containerStatuses field)."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Differences from regular containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"Init containers support all the fields and features of app containers, including resource limits, volumes, and security settings. However, the resource requests and limits for an init container are handled differently, as documented in Resource sharing within containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"Regular init containers (in other words: excluding sidecar containers) do not support the lifecycle, livenessProbe, readinessProbe, or startupProbe fields. Init containers must run to completion before the Pod can be ready; sidecar containers continue running during a Pod's lifetime, and do support some probes. See sidecar container for further details about sidecar containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you specify multiple init containers for a Pod, kubelet runs each init container sequentially. Each init container must succeed before the next can run. When all of the init containers have run to completion, kubelet initializes the application containers for the Pod and runs them as usual."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Differences from sidecar containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"Init containers run and complete their tasks before the main application container starts. Unlike sidecar containers, init containers are not continuously running alongside the main containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"Init containers run to completion sequentially, and the main container does not start until all the init containers have successfully completed."},{"kind":"p","text":"init containers do not support lifecycle, livenessProbe, readinessProbe, or startupProbe whereas sidecar containers support all these probes to control their lifecycle."},{"kind":"p","text":"Init containers share the same resources (CPU, memory, network) with the main application containers but do not interact directly with them. They can, however, use shared volumes for data exchange."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Using init containers"},{"kind":"p","text":"Because init containers have separate images from app containers, they have some advantages for start-up related code:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Init containers can contain utilities or custom code for setup that are not present in an app image. For example, there is no need to make an image FROM another image just to use a tool like sed, awk, python, or dig during setup."},{"kind":"li","text":"The application image builder and deployer roles can work independently without the need to jointly build a single app image."},{"kind":"li","text":"Init containers can run with a different view of the filesystem than app containers in the same Pod. Consequently, they can be given access to Secrets that app containers cannot access."},{"kind":"li","text":"Because init containers run to completion before any app containers start, init containers offer a mechanism to block or delay app container startup until a set of preconditions are met. Once preconditions are met, all of the app containers in a Pod can start in parallel."},{"kind":"li","text":"Init containers can securely run utilities or custom code that would otherwise make an app container image less secure. By keeping unnecessary tools separate you can limit the attack surface of your app container image."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Examples"},{"kind":"p","text":"Here are some ideas for how to use init containers:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Wait for a Service to be created, using a shell one-line command like:"}],"lemmas":{"inits":["inits","init"],"containers":["container","containers"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"provides":["provides","provide"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"overviews":["overview","overviews"],"specialized":["specialized","specialize"],"specializeds":["specializeds","specialized"],"sp